    "result_text": "The full test suite passes with `BUILD SUCCESS`. `AcceptanceTest` is the only test class (plus the abstract base), and it's green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `namesakeCount` field on owner creation. When a new owner is created via `POST /api/owners`, the response (and subsequent `GET /api/owners/{id}`) now includes `namesakeCount` = the number of owners that already existed *before* this create sharing the same `firstName` and `lastName`, compared case-insensitively.\n\nChanges:\n\n1. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 computes the count before saving the new owner and sets it on the entity, via a new `countNamesakes(firstName, lastName)` helper that scans existing owners with a null-safe case-insensitive comparison. It's computed before `saveOwner`, so it reflects only pre-existing owners.\n\n2. **`Owner` entity** \u2014 added the persisted `namesakeCount` field (`namesake_count` column) with getter/setter, following the existing pattern of `customerCode`/`householdId`.\n\n3. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added `namesakeCount` as a `readOnly` int32 property on the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it and MapStruct maps it by name automatically.\n\n4. **Schema SQL** (hsqldb/h2/mysql/postgres) \u2014 added the `namesake_count` column to the `owners` table so the entity persists and reads back correctly. The tests run on hsqldb.\n\nThe value is persisted at create time so the household/duplicate rules are unaffected, and reading the owner back returns the snapshot count as the test expects (owner \"b\" returns `namesakeCount = 1` after \"a\" with the same name was created first).",
